#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171688 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "416faff8-3422-5bb0-9062-571ccfb18093" :question/math-academy-id "e-10094" :question/problem "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7  x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_1,y_1), \\: \\ldots, \\: (x_{8},y_{8})$ of $n=8$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $1.25,$ and $S_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 = 42,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $6$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7  x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope $\\beta_1$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] }^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=1.6+0.7  x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 = 0.7.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{6,0.05} = 1.943.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]  = 1.943 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.25}{42}} \\approx 0.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] =  0.7 \\pm 0.3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171696 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "4d33f1a3-c51f-5a97-a498-def7a8364d81" :question/math-academy-id "e-11098" :question/problem "| **Hours ($x$)** | **$4$** | **$4.5$** | **$5.5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Grades ($y$)** | $62$ | $65$ | $77$ | $81$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours each of four students spent studying for a biology exam and the grades they finally obtained on that exam. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=5,$ and $\\textrm{MSE}=1.375,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.92) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x = 21.3 + 10x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope $\\beta_1$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] }^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 = 10.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.92) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{2,0.05} = 2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing $S_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(4-5)^{2}+(4.5-5)^{2}+(5.5-5)^{2}+(6-5)^{2} \\\\  & =1+0.25+0.25+1 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]  = 2.92 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.375}{2.5}} \\approx 2.2\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = 10 \\pm 2.2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "555a7349-2df2-538a-a305-59c88e1a33fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554033} {:answer/id #uuid "6f5a082e-ad3d-51f6-8adc-48f4994128fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554092} {:answer/id #uuid "755634f2-688a-5a80-a8c4-78381f6a4f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554107} {:answer/id #uuid "790f4579-99a1-5019-8dd1-e9c8506783f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554113} {:answer/id #uuid "8344100f-13ac-5416-8e25-9c878812b0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f5a082e-ad3d-51f6-8adc-48f4994128fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554092} :answer-field/id #uuid "10137e41-2459-5b06-b3a4-3230a1046efa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ac4a55b-9276-584c-aabe-f3000169b367" :question/math-academy-id "q-143260" :question/problem "| **Age ($x$)** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Height ($y$)** | $95$ | $106$ | $115$ | $125$ |\n\nThe table above gives the ages, in years, and the corresponding heights, in centimeters, of four children. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=80.6+5x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=6,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.35,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>4.303)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=80.6+5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=80.6+5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>4.303)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.025}=4.303.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(3-6)^{2}+(5-6)^{2}+(7-6)^{2}+(9-6)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+1+9 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=4.303\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.35}{20}}≈0.6\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=5\\pm 0.6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52af52d6-c43f-5772-9249-56cb2e062ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8\\pm 0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554029} {:answer/id #uuid "804a8e29-21c2-50e1-85dd-8bf5ea96d9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8\\pm 0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554126} {:answer/id #uuid "8182d4e1-5154-5473-a21d-13951e34b26e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8\\pm 0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554129} {:answer/id #uuid "923c151e-4c0c-5138-872f-79d725e9b5a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3\\pm 0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554191} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7cbdf0-9605-52c2-aebb-1c3a606aa1d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5\\pm 0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52af52d6-c43f-5772-9249-56cb2e062ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8\\pm 0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554029} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dbbd93d-093b-57e4-bfc1-ad59a17bdad5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42368253-8090-5197-973b-1010d704c08f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159367" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4+1.8x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{12},{y}_{12})$ of $n=12$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $11.5,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=143,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.812)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $10$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4+1.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=3.4+1.8x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=1.8.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.812)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{10,0.05}=1.812.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.812\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{11.5}{143}}≈0.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.8\\pm 0.5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "278a0133-b0b4-5d1a-81b8-49903ddff8ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553944} {:answer/id #uuid "508865b1-77ef-551a-a36f-b6776f0a785e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554025} {:answer/id #uuid "5db73e1c-4ac3-5171-b6a5-b6b7072792bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554053} {:answer/id #uuid "80cf0bd5-fc8a-5eed-99c9-1117dae18dbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554127} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b5686d-bff3-5555-ae5d-0690a2f7e5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4b5686d-bff3-5555-ae5d-0690a2f7e5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.4" :db/