, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=1-2.6x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=1-2.6x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-2.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.262)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{9,0.025}=2.262.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.262\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.78}{110}}≈0.2\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-2.6\\pm 0.2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f51304-e3b6-5932-ad51-bd19b9045d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553867} {:answer/id #uuid "0a29929b-8aeb-5e21-9031-f1ccdab60a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553887} {:answer/id #uuid "49fa42a9-46e5-5910-9c55-1b9972e31902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 1.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554011} {:answer/id #uuid "d7bc35a1-e944-560a-a330-d31779067a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554340} {:answer/id #uuid "db665bb1-2442-5540-9fe3-cca6ff6884bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00f51304-e3b6-5932-ad51-bd19b9045d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553867} :answer-field/id #uuid "04602db9-708b-5fcf-bfdb-6de19a2849cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f28f1c0e-4ffd-5dee-98b7-1c475d5129ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-159398" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4-0.6x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{9},{y}_{9})$ of $n=9$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $1.16,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=240,$ find a $90%$  confidence interval for the slope of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.895)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $7$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4-0.6x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=3.4-0.6x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-0.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.895)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{7,0.05}=1.895.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.895\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{1.16}{240}}≈0.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-0.6\\pm 0.1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "337b83df-33f6-5e1a-bb43-103241a1e6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\pm 0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553960} {:answer/id #uuid "812e97bc-01ca-5b39-9e67-dd0fed627e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\pm 0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554128} {:answer/id #uuid "95ee6076-b2f9-5bbc-b329-206a5101eee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.8\\pm 0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554200} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a799fa-6ed7-5277-8e8a-013cdcf480ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2\\pm 0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554223} {:answer/id #uuid "c235c63d-67de-5b7d-ba35-67ffec0716b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2\\pm 0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "812e97bc-01ca-5b39-9e67-dd0fed627e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\pm 0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554128} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0c08eb5-9c27-5ce5-b185-ad184aa151bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "764e52d5-673e-5136-a2ff-4fbb20c5aad9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159454" :question/problem "| **Age ($x$)** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Price ($y$)** | $10.2$ | $9$ | $8.1$ | $5$ |\n\nThe table above shows the ages, in years, and corresponding selling prices, in thousands of dollars, of four car models as advertised in a newspaper classifieds last month. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=12.7-x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=4.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.456,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.92)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=12.7-x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=12.7-x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.92)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(2-4.5)^{2}+(4-4.5)^{2}+(5-4.5)^{2}+(7-4.5)^{2} \\\\  & =6.25+0.25+0.25+6.25 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.92\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.456}{13}}≈0.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-1\\pm 0.5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f5b1a94-4fcd-578f-9a4a-88a6dfb266da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554021} {:answer/id #uuid "82b33e0f-3da2-5ac3-84ca-7016f07baa0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\pm 0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554131} {:answer/id #uuid "8eac7c38-eeac-5c2f-b55d-b4f3d6f40198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\pm 0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554174} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd9b13d-b9d6-5c94-bbd9-97339852c840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\pm 0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554210} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cd1b87-8a86-58a2-85a0-b0c99abe0e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bd9b13d-b9d6-5c94-bbd9-97339852c840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\pm 0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554210} :answer-field/id #uuid "086dbd14-306d-5d79-b710-e38344655d09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2bc734f-9a4b-543f-ac01-84af782ea390" :question/math-academy-id "q-159518" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$67$** | **$70$** | **$73$** | **$76$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Shoe size ($y$)** | $8.5$ | $9$ | $11$ | $13$ |\n\nThe table above shows the heights, in inches, and corresponding shoe sizes of four randomly selected men. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=71.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.338,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.  \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.92)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-26.6+0.5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=0.5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.92)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(67-71.5)^{2}+(70-71.5)^{2}+(73-71.5)^{2}+(76-71.5)^{2} \\\\  & =20.25+2.25+2.25+20.25 \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.92\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.338}{45}}≈0.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=0.5\\pm 0.3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a86111c-7d04-5aa8-9f71-1296e12f0b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553889} {:answer/id #uuid "4d17947d-e25a-5ba5-90a6-ea44648fe558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554019} {:answer/id #uuid "770545bb-976b-5b91-a99e-1a334310037a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554109} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a2715c-198e-5860-9080-d610e3c815c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 1.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554228} {:answer/id #uuid "d95d02e9-5565-5b9e-8b79-42153a723931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2a2715c-198e-5860-9080-d610e3c815c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 1.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554228} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce3aef4c-bd71-5e3d-813c-31f1dae5f512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "939b2026-f5f3-5f3b-a15d-5ba46809dfda" :question/math-academy-id "q-159541" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$60$** | **$62$** | **$66$** | **$68$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **IQ  ($y$)** | $110$ | $106$ | $108$ | $102$ |\n\nThe table above gives the heights, in inches, and the corresponding scores on an IQ test of four high school students. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=64,$ and $\\text{MSE}=7.7,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>4.303)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=151.3-0.7x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-0.7.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>4.303)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.025}=4.303.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(60-64)^{2}+(62-64)^{2}+(66-64)^{2}+(68-64)^{2} \\\\  & =16+4+4+16 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=4.303\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{7.7}{40}}≈1.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-0.7\\pm 1.9\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dbd2b70-b3ee-509d-be90-3ff4a1a62f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1\\pm 0.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553922} {:answer/id #uuid "26a57e1e-5b7b-57c8-aa04-88dc12913fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1\\pm 0.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553941} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac845ba-d2be-5059-8e0d-b39565e56bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1\\pm 0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554164} {:answer/id #uuid "b12dd743-8c8e-57ed-8222-bc18a8d6084f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1\\pm 0.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554261} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b413f8-d2fb-5f5c-af45-47160c32ee73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1\\pm 0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ac845ba-d2be-5059-8e0d-b39565e56bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1\\pm 0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554164} :answer-field/id #uuid "618d8cc5-09ee-52cf-bb55-128c57e46ade" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d548f76-b08c-5cf0-ae75-59db6390b2b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-159571" :question/problem "| ** Production  $\\,$ ($x$)** | **$24$** | **$27$** | **$30$** | **$31$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ** Price per $kg$  ($y$) ** | $2$ | $1.6$ | $1.4$ | $1.4$ |\n\nThe table above shows wheat production, in tons, and the average price of a kilogram of flour, in dollars, for a given country over the last four years. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=4-0.1x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=28,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.007,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.   \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.92)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-4-0.1x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=4-0.1x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-0.1.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.92)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(24-28)^{2}+(27-28)^{2}+(30-28)^{2}+(31-28)^{2} \\\\  & =16+1+4+9 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.92\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.007}{30}}≈0.04\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-0.1\\pm 0.04\n$$"}]}
